from data import *
def build(R):
    R.h1("3. First Approach: UAMM-1")
    R.p("Having surveyed the algorithms of Chapter 2, we observed that each solves one part of the problem in isolation: paging manages layout, H2O scores token importance, quantisation shrinks tensors, and selection skips reads. The first system we built, **UAMM-1** (Unified Adaptive Memory Manager, version 1), was an attempt to join the first two into one memory manager for an edge processor. This chapter describes everything UAMM-1 contained.", align="j")
    R.h2("3.1 Components")
    R.h3("3.1.1 Paged block store")
    R.p("The KV cache was divided into fixed-size blocks of consecutive tokens, as in PagedAttention. A block table recorded, for each logical block, the memory level that held it. A block was the unit of allocation, movement and scoring; per-token bookkeeping was avoided.", align="j")
    R.h3("3.1.2 H2O-style importance")
    R.p("Following H2O, each token accumulated the attention weight it received at every decode step. The importance of a block was a function of the accumulated weights of its tokens. The idea is that a small set of heavy-hitter tokens receives most of the attention, so blocks containing them should be kept close to the processor.", align="j")
    R.h3("3.1.3 Three-level placement")
    R.p("Three memory levels were modelled: a fast level near the processor, DRAM and a slow non-volatile level (flash). Each block received a combined score from three signals:", align="j")
    R.eq("score = 0.60 · importance + 0.25 · recency + 0.15 · access frequency")
    R.p("and was assigned a level by two fixed thresholds: a block with score ≥ 0.68 went to the fast level, a block with 0.35 ≤ score < 0.68 to DRAM, and a block below 0.35 to flash. When a level was full the block was pushed down to the next level (cascade demotion); when no level had room, the block was discarded.", align="j")
    R.callout("Worked example", ["A block has importance 0.90, recency 0.50 and access frequency 0.40. Score = 0.60 × 0.90 + 0.25 × 0.50 + 0.15 × 0.40 = 0.54 + 0.125 + 0.06 = 0.725. Since 0.725 ≥ 0.68 it is placed in the fast level.",
                                    "A second block has importance 0.20, recency 0.30 and frequency 0.10: score = 0.12 + 0.075 + 0.015 = 0.21 < 0.35, so it goes to flash. If flash is also full, the block is dropped."])
    R.h3("3.1.4 Hardware decision engine")
    R.p("A small Verilog module computed the weighted score and the level decision for one block at a time, intended to offload the arithmetic from the processor. It was checked by a testbench with three input vectors.", align="j")
    R.h3("3.1.5 Software model")
    R.p("A Python simulator reproduced the policy, advanced the H2O scores token by token, and estimated latency from the level each block resided in.", align="j")
    R.h2("3.2 What UAMM-1 achieved")
    R.p("UAMM-1 showed that importance-driven tiering is workable: hot blocks stay near the processor, cold ones move away, and the decision can be made cheaply in hardware. It also supplied the structure that UAMM-2 keeps: blocks, a block table, a hot/warm/cold classifier and its RTL scorer. For this reason the original threshold policy was retained in the code as a baseline (`ThresholdPlanner`), so that UAMM-1 and UAMM-2 can be compared on identical inputs.", align="j")
